---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import StructuredData from '../components/StructuredData.astro';
import {DRAGON_COLORS,TAIL_TYPES} from '../lib/dragons';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { isPublishedCharacter } from '../lib/publication';
import { PAGE_TITLES } from '../lib/page-titles';
const characterPages = (await getCollection('characters', isPublishedCharacter))
  .filter(entry => entry.data.contentStatus === 'published')
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.slugName.localeCompare(b.data.slugName));
const faqs=[['What does this page index?','It indexes seven color labels and six tail labels used in the site navigation.'],['Does a quiz result establish a dragon’s personality?','No. Quiz personalities are original interpretations for entertainment.']];
---
<BaseLayout title={PAGE_TITLES['/dragons/']} description="Browse seven dragon color labels and six tail labels in a concise Empyrean vocabulary index." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Dragon Types',href:'/dragons/'}]}>
<h1>Fourth Wing Dragon Color and Tail Index</h1>
<p class="lead">Seven color words and six compounds ending in tail are vocabulary labels. The notes follow literary color tradition and English word formation. A heading here is not a verified dragon from the books.</p>
<div class="grid cards"><section class="bordered"><h2>Color labels</h2><ul>{DRAGON_COLORS.map(c=><li id={c.id}>{c.name}</li>)}</ul></section><section class="bordered"><h2>Tail labels</h2><ul>{TAIL_TYPES.map(t=><li id={t.id}>{t.name}</li>)}</ul></section></div>
<section class="prose">
<h2>Color words in literary tradition</h2>
<p>Color words carry moods in poetry, heraldry, and seasonal description because readers already know their ordinary associations. The same color can mean warmth in one sentence and warning in the next. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, these headings establish no temperament, hierarchy, or creature.</p>
<h3>Black</h3>
<p>In literary English, black often sits beside night, ink, and whatever cannot be seen clearly. Writers use it for concealment, mourning, or a formal darkness. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, a dark color is a shorthand for the unfamiliar, not a judgment of character.</p>
<h3>Blue</h3>
<p>Blue has a long association with sky, deep water, and distance. Cool description often suggests reflection, restraint, or weather that has not yet broken. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, the word names a color, not a disposition shared by every blue thing.</p>
<h3>Green</h3>
<p>Green commonly recalls leaves, growth, and renewal, and it can also suggest envy or an unfamiliar wilderness. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, the surrounding sentence decides the mood. The color itself does not.</p>
<h3>Red</h3>
<p>Red can evoke heat, urgency, courage, ceremony, or danger, which is why a single red object often becomes the point of emphasis. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, that intensity is a stylistic habit, not a rule that assigns one personality to the color.</p>
<h3>Orange</h3>
<p>Orange sits between red and yellow in ordinary color talk. Writers borrow that in-between place for warmth, autumn, embers, or a changing horizon. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, the word can enliven a passage without implying how any creature behaves.</p>
<h3>Brown</h3>
<p>Brown brings earth, wood, leather, and weathered materials to mind, and descriptive writing uses it to ground a scene in soil or handmade objects. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, those associations can feel rustic. They do not classify a being.</p>
<h3>Golden</h3>
<p>Golden describes a goldlike color, often tied in literary English to sunlight, splendor, wealth, or ceremony. Iridescent describes shifting colors as the light changes. As a general fantasy convention, not an Empyrean setting detail, the two words stay distinct, and neither documents a hide or a rank.</p>
</section>
<section class="prose">
<h2>Compounds ending in tail</h2>
<p>In an endocentric English compound the last element is the head and the first element modifies it. Closed spellings join the parts. Hyphenated spellings keep a boundary. These labels put an object or animal in front of tail. The pattern explains the word. It does not establish anatomy, behavior, or a fictional classification.</p>
<h3>Daggertail</h3>
<p>Daggertail is dagger plus tail, written as one closed compound. Dagger modifies the head noun tail with a blade-related image. The spelling is a naming pattern, not a physical description from any book.</p>
<h3>Morningstar-tail</h3>
<p>Morningstar-tail joins morningstar, a spiked striking weapon, with tail. The hyphen keeps the boundary visible. A weapon name can modify a fantasy label. The compound does not measure a tail or assign the label to a creature.</p>
<h3>Swordtail</h3>
<p>Swordtail joins sword and tail with no space and no hyphen. Tail is the head, and sword contributes the image of a long blade. The compound can suggest a resemblance. The word formation stops there.</p>
<h3>Scorpion-tail</h3>
<p>Scorpion-tail links an animal noun with tail and keeps the hyphen, so both parts stay recognizable. The animal image sits in the modifier. It does not establish anatomy, behavior, or membership in a breed.</p>
<h3>Club-tail</h3>
<p>Club-tail combines club, here a striking implement, with tail. The hyphen shows which word modifies the other. Reusing an ordinary noun inside an invented label is a stylistic choice, not a measurement.</p>
<h3>Feathertail</h3>
<p>Feathertail is feather plus tail, a closed compound. Feather contributes a material or shape association to the head noun. A linguistic reading cannot establish what a fictional creature looks like, or how any story uses the label.</p>
</section>
<p class="source-note">Book information: <a href="https://www.entangledpublishing.com/books/fourth-wing">Entangled Publishing — Fourth Wing</a>. The publisher's book description provides series context, rather than a source for the color and tail lists.</p>
{characterPages.length > 0 && <section aria-labelledby="related-pages">
  <h2 id="related-pages">Related pages</h2>
  <ul>
    {characterPages.map(entry => entry.data.contentStatus === 'published' && <li><a href={`/characters/${entry.data.slugName}/`}>{entry.data.title}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
</section>}
<section class="prose"><h2>Common questions</h2>{faqs.map(([q,a])=><details><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}<p>Try the <a href="/quiz/dragon-type/">dragon color quiz</a> or read the <a href="/guide-to-dragonkind/">guide to official and fan resources</a>.</p></section>
<StructuredData data={{'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'ItemList',numberOfItems:DRAGON_COLORS.length,itemListElement:DRAGON_COLORS.map((c,i)=>({'@type':'ListItem',position:i+1,name:c.name,url:'https://fourthwingdragon.com/dragons/#'+c.id}))}}/>
<StructuredData data={{'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage',mainEntity:faqs.map(([name,text])=>({'@type':'Question',name,acceptedAnswer:{'@type':'Answer',text}}))}}/>
</BaseLayout>
